# Rendering method guardrails

These constraints protect the core value of this project (`Image Based RT`): a specific
image-based, mirrored-camera reflection technique. Follow them for any change touching
rendering (`src/implementation.js`, `src/main.js`, `src/scenes/*`, shaders, or the demo).

## Do not change the inherent method
- Do **not** replace, rewrite, or fundamentally alter the ray-tracing-like reflection
  method (depth shadow pass → mirrored-camera reflection image → water/mirror composite).
- The reflection must remain a **per-frame mirrored render**, not a substitute algorithm.
- Preserve the public API and pipeline stages in `src/implementation.js`.

## Only optimization changes are allowed
- Permitted changes are those that make the existing method **faster / cheaper** while
  producing the **same or better** visual result (e.g. batching, culling, cheaper math,
  target reuse, memory savings, quality-preset tuning).
- Do **not** introduce feature changes that trade away the method for performance.

## No permanently baked images
- Do **not** bake the reflection/scene into a static image that is captured once and reused
  indefinitely. Permanently pre-baked/frozen images conflict with the interactive,
  view-dependent quality of the original method and are **not allowed**.
- Temporal reuse that already exists (skipping/refreshing shadow & reflection targets when
  the view is stable, with dirty keys for light pose, neon intensity, and `contentVersion`)
  is fine — it is bounded reuse, not a permanent bake.

## Keep quality high
- Visual quality must not regress. Reflections, shadows, and the water composite should look
  as good as or better than before any change.
- If an optimization would reduce fidelity, gate it behind the appropriate quality preset
  (low / balanced / high) rather than degrading the default experience.
- Verify changes visually in a WebGL2 browser (see `AGENTS.md` and `PROJECT.md`).
